  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-magpie-connector",
      "text": "English · [简体中文](README.zh.md)\n\n*Top: model picker grouped by upstream provider. Middle: Settings → Magpie — the connection line, the sidebar usage panel (toggle plus auto-refresh interval in minutes) and per-provider model visibility, default effort and usage cards. Bottom: the resident sidebar usage card (click its header to expand every window). Captured from an isolated DSH host (English UI) with a local mock Magpie gateway; gateway address, models, account and readings are synthetic.*"
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-magpie-connector",
      "text": "<!-- problem -->\n**Connect a self-hosted Magpie gateway to DeepSeek Harness with one provider route per upstream owner: models grouped by upstream provider on stock DSH, usage cards (you can spend a Codex reset from them), and a per-session fast mode.** Magpie keeps managing logins, accounts, routing and provider egress; this plugin adds the DSH side."
    },
    {
      "section": "What you get",
      "text": "| Capability | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Gateway lines** (basic) | Each line has its own URL and gateway key. The model catalog comes from the gateway's `GET /models`; Responses-capable models use Responses, everything else uses Chat Completions. Text, tool round trips, images, usage, cancellation and DSH-managed retries go through. |\n| **Check a line before saving** (0.4.0) | Every line card has “Check line”: one real `GET /models` with that card's address, access mode and key (empty credentials fall back to the line's saved values), rendered as a read-only snapshot — result kind, latency, model count and, when the host has a cached catalog with a sync time, the model ids extra to or missing from that cache. It never changes the active line, writes credentials/settings, syncs the catalog or registers a route; the diff is against the *cached* catalog, so it never claims to match the current line. |\n| **Automatic multi-line failover** (signature) | Empty on first install; normally one line; add backup lines and let the order decide priority. Before every gateway operation the plugin picks the first reachable complete line with a ≤600ms TCP probe (10s positive / 15s negative in-me"
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "```sh\ndsh plugin --profile web add dsh-magpie-connector\n```\n\nRestart DSH and refresh the Web page. Inside [ohmydsh](https://github.com/prgrmrwy/ohmydsh) you can instead enable the local `dsh-magpie-connector` manifest entry and rebuild. Tested with DSH `0.2.0-rc.2`, Web UI, Node.js `>=22.19`."
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "> **0.3.0 BREAKING upgrade**: the single `dsh-magpie-connector` route is retired. Sessions and the default model saved on it no longer resolve; pick the model again from a `Magpie (…)` group. Routes that depend on the exact route id elsewhere (e.g. subagent routing, egress allowlists) must list the new `dsh-magpie-connector-<owner>` ids."
    },
    {
      "section": "Configure",
      "text": "1. In **line 1**, enter the gateway API URL **including `/v1`**, for example `http://192.168.1.20:3425/v1`, and that line's gateway key. The line list, addresses, visibility and default efforts live at `$DSH_HOME/plugins/dsh-magpie-connector/settings.json` — keys and Access values are never written there or returned by the settings API.\n2. For multiple ingresses press **Add backup line**; each line has its own address and key. Use the move-up/move-down buttons to change priority (number 1 wins). Saving keeps only the active line expanded; the 4×4 green marker after its number shows the current line, and the info icon explains priority and automatic fallback.\n3. Inside each line's **Advanced connection** choose Direct or Cloudflare Access. LAN and Tailscale are Direct; Cloudflare Access requires an HTTPS line and switching back to Direct clears that line's two Access credentials on save. Cloudflare Free/Pro returns 524 when the origin sends nothing for 100 seconds.\n4. Saving synchronizes the catalog; **Refresh model list** synchronizes again, and there is no catalog polling."
    },
    {
      "section": "Configure",
      "text": "> **0.2.0 BREAKING upgrade**: settings are now a single `lines[]` format. A legacy single-address `settings.json` (top-level `baseUrl`) and the legacy fixed credential refs (`MAGPIE_API_KEY` / `MAGPIE_ACCESS_*`) are **not read or migrated**; re-add your lines and enter each line's key/Access in Settings. Model visibility and default efforts are preserved; old credential records can be deleted manually in DSH credentials. Back up `settings.json` and the old refs first if you want to keep a rollback path."
    },
    {
      "section": "Configure",
      "text": "Need another method — custom headers, mTLS, another zero-trust gateway? **Issues and pull requests are welcome**; describe your deployment and the gateway version."
    },
    {
      "section": "Where things happen",
      "text": "| Where | What you do |\n|---|---|\n| **Model picker** | Search across group headings, model names and full ids. Each group is a real route `dsh-magpie-connector-<owner>`; model ids stay the gateway's full ids, and visibility keys and default effort are unchanged. |\n| **Model menu groups** | `owned_by` (lower-cased; 1–48 chars of `a-z0-9._-`) picks the route and the label `Magpie (<Owner>)` with the first letter capitalised. Missing or invalid ownership goes to `Magpie (Other)` instead of guessing from the model name. Owners seen once are remembered in `settings.json` `knownOwners` (max 32) so their routes exist right after a restart; the models appear once the first catalog sync finishes. An owner that disappears keeps its (empty, hidden) route. Clearing `knownOwners` by hand invalidates selections saved on the removed routes. If route registration fails at start-up (another plugin owns the id), Settings shows the error and DSH must be restarted after resolving it. |\n| **Composer speed control** | Shows only for the current model when the gateway advertises the `priority` tier (on current Magpie: the Codex/GPT models), and updates as soon as the selection changes. Hover the model me"
    },
    {
      "section": "Spending a reset",
      "text": "**Usage limit resets** folds out every credit the account holds. Only the credit that expires first carries **Use…**: it stays inert until the weekly window reaches 80%, and confirming spends that one after the two-step check. Expiry times are right-aligned and the rows line up with the section title."
    },
    {
      "section": "Behavior worth knowing",
      "text": "- **Failed requests are retried by DSH, not replayed by the plugin.** The adapter declares the same `llm-retry` policy as `dsh-plugin-subscriptions` (10 attempts, exponential backoff from 1s to 60s, ±20% jitter, honoring `Retry-After`). `TIMEOUT`, `TRANSPORT`, `SERVER`, `RATE_LIMIT` and `EMPTY_RESPONSE` are retryable; 401/403, exhausted quota, context overflow and other 4xx are not.\n- **The default output budget reserves room for compaction.** The gateway's `max_output_tokens` is the model's capacity, not the per-request default. The default budget handed to DSH is `min(capacity, max(1, window/4, window/2 − 65536))`, leaving context space for DSH compaction; an explicit `maxTokens` is unaffected. Models with a window below 104,026 tokens follow the same formula but are not guaranteed to pass DSH's compaction checks.\n- **Only silence ends a stream.** Headers, SSE events and heartbeats all count as liveness; 300 seconds without any data ends the request with `TIMEOUT` (message contains `MAGPIE_STREAM_TIMEOUT`). Long reasoning survives as long as the gateway keeps the stream alive.\n- **Connection changes do not abort work.** A new address or key immediately hides the old catalog and c"
    },
    {
      "section": "Code map",
      "text": "Entry: [index.ts](src/index.ts). Adapter and request/stream mapping: [adapter.ts](src/host/adapter.ts). Routes: [routes.ts](src/host/routes.ts). Transport allowlist: [transport.ts](src/host/transport.ts). Settings page: [SettingsPage.ts](src/client/SettingsPage.ts). The diagram source, with credential-free repository evidence: [architecture.en.json](docs/architecture.en.json)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Development",
      "text": "In a standalone checkout of this repository:\n\n```sh\nnpm install\nnpm run typecheck\nnpm test\nnpm run build\n\nInside the ohmydsh monorepo, install once at the root and add `--workspace=dsh-magpie-connector` to the last three commands. Tests use local mock gateways and the real wire library; no real key is required. Build output is not committed. Both `prepare` and `prepack` build the package."
    },
    {
      "section": "Removal",
      "text": "<!-- section: removal -->\n\n```sh\ndsh plugin --profile web remove dsh-magpie-connector\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Removal",
